Now it is clear that *E, F,* and *G* and *A, H, K,* and *B* are continuously proportional in the ratio of *C* to *D.* And, since *A, H, K,* and *B* are continuously proportional, and *A* measures *B* and *G* therefore it also measures *H.* [!just VIII.11; VIII.12; VIII.7]

And *A* is to *H* as *C* is to *D,* therefore *C* also measures *D.* [!just VII.Def.20]

* * *

Next, let *C* measure *D.*

I say that *A* also measures *B.*

With the same construction, we can prove in a similar manner that *A, H, K,* and *B* are continuously proportional in the ratio of *C* to *D.* And, since *C* measures *D,* and *C* is to *D* as *A* is to *H,* therefore *A* also measures *H,* so that *A* measures *B* also. [!just VII.Def.20]

Therefore, *if a cubic number measures a cubic number, then the side also measures the side; and, if the side measures the side, then the cube also measures the cube.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

*c*<sup>3</sup> divides *d*<sup>3</sup> if and only if *c* divides *d*.

This proposition is used to prove its contrapositive, @VIII.17.
